        <p class="profile-detail-region">Battle · Europe</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battle of the Teutoburg Forest</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">8 to 11 September 9 CE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">Possibly near Kalkriese, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osnabruck">Osnabrück</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>; the site was long unidentified and the older theories placed it elsewhere</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> under the governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-quinctilius-varus">Publius Quinctilius Varus</a>, with three legions (about 15,000 men) and nine small auxiliary units (about 4,500 men), though estimates of his army generally range from 20,000 to 30,000; an alliance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanic-peoples">Germanic peoples</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arminius">Arminius</a> of the Cherusci, whose numbers are a guess: Murdoch suggests 25,000, Wells 17,000 to 100,000, Delbrück 20,000 to 30,000</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Roman army was ambushed and destroyed over several days and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-quinctilius-varus">Varus</a> killed himself. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> lost its forts east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, and the Rhine became the frontier.</span></div>
        </div>
