    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arminius">Arminius</a> was born into the Cherusci, was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> as a hostage, received a Roman education, joined the Roman cavalry and commanded auxiliaries, so he knew how the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> fought and was trusted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-quinctilius-varus">Varus</a>.</li><li>The Roman column was strung out over 15 to 20 kilometres of forest and then caught on a strip of ground only about 100 metres wide between a hill and a bog, in front of a wall the Germanic warriors had built.</li><li>After the defeat the legion numbers XVII, XVIII and XIX were not used again, and later Roman campaigns under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanicus">Germanicus</a> recovered two of the three lost eagles in 14 and 15 CE and the third in 41 CE.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-quinctilius-varus">Varus</a>'s army was paid on the morning of 7 September, and the large number of coins handed out became an archaeological marker: Tony Clunn's metal detector found coins of Augustus's reign at Kalkriese in 1987.</p></aside>
    </section>

