    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">8 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Drusus defeats the Cherusci, and the boy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arminius">Arminius</a> is taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> as a hostage</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">7 CE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-quinctilius-varus">Varus</a> takes command of the legions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania">Germania</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">Spring 9 CE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-quinctilius-varus">Varus</a> leaves winter quarters and sets up a summer camp, probably near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minden">Minden</a>, in Cherusci lands</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">September 9 CE</span><span class="rf-text">The army moves out for a campaign against the Angrivarii, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arminius">Arminius</a> leaves it</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">September 9 CE</span><span class="rf-text">The Germanic alliance attacks the army in the forest and at Kalkriese, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-quinctilius-varus">Varus</a> kills himself</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">9 CE</span><span class="rf-text">The fort of Aliso holds out for weeks before its garrison breaks through to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">14 to 16 CE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanicus">Germanicus</a> campaigns east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and recovers two of the lost eagles</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">21 CE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arminius">Arminius</a> is poisoned by fellow Germans</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-the-teutoburg-forest" data-v="910b19a07f" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The Bructeri strike the strung-out column</b> The Roman army moved through thick forest, its line of march stretched over 15 to 20 kilometres. The Bructeri attacked along its length with javelins, a brief skirmish meant to tire the Romans and damage their supplies. The Angrivarii lay hidden at Kalkriese.</li><li><b>The Bructeri withdraw and the Romans camp</b> The Bructeri withdrew quickly. A torrential downpour left the Romans unable to advance, and Varus ordered the army to make camp. Losses reported were light, but the baggage trains and scout cavalry were shown to be vulnerable.</li><li><b>A night march into the second trap</b> The Romans marched at night to escape and went into another trap set at the foot of Kalkriese Hill. There the open strip they could march along was squeezed to a gap of about 100 metres between the woods and the swampland at the edge of the Great Bog.</li><li><b>The Romans try to storm the wall</b> The road was blocked by a trench and an earthen wall built along its side, which let the Germanic alliance attack from cover. The Romans made a desperate attempt to storm the wall and failed.</li><li><b>Vala rides off and is overtaken</b> The highest-ranking officer next to Varus, Numonius Vala, abandoned the troops by riding off with the cavalry. Germanic cavalry overtook him and he was killed shortly afterwards, according to Velleius Paterculus.</li><li><b>The Germanic warriors storm the field</b> The Germanic warriors stormed the field and cut down the disintegrating Roman forces. Varus killed himself. By later accounts some officers were sacrificed or ransomed and some common soldiers enslaved.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/julius-caesar.jpg" alt="Julius Caesar" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Retrato_de_Julio_C%C3%A9sar_(26724093101)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ángel M. Felicísimo from Mérida, España, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> twice crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallic-wars">Gallic Wars</a> (58 to 50 BCE), but the engagements were inconclusive. After Augustus reorganised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> into three provinces, policy towards the Germanic lands remains unclear. One theory holds that he meant the Rhine to be the northern border, another that the border was fluid and the troops were there to keep order. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clades-lolliana">Lollian disaster</a> of 16 BCE led to a reorganisation of the legions, and Drusus campaigned against the Germans from 11 to 9 BCE until he died in a riding accident. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> took command in 8 BCE, and Lucius Domitius Ahenobarbus later crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>, the first and last Roman general to do so.</p><p>By the turn of the century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> felt secure, reassured by cross-border trade and relative peace. In early 6 CE a huge army of 13 legions and about 100,000 men set out against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maroboduus">Maroboduus</a>, king of the Marcomanni, a tribe of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suebi">Suebi</a>. Tiberius had to break off when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-illyrian-revolt">Great Illyrian Revolt</a> began in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>. He recognised Maroboduus as king and sent eight legions to crush the rebellion, which lasted nearly four years. Only three legions remained to face the Germanic tribesmen, and the weakened Roman position gave an opportunity for a Germanic rebellion.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of the Teutoburg Forest&#8221; cites where it covers Geopolitical situation, A simple Roman campaign, Germanicus' campaign against the Germanic coalition, Later campaigns:</p><ul><li>Michael McNally, Teutoburg Forest, AD 9: the destruction of Varus and his legions (Osprey Publishing, 2011)</li><li>Catherine Gilliver, Caesar's Gallic wars, 58–50 BC (Routledge, 2003)</li><li>Peter S. Wells, The Battle That Stopped Rome: Emperor Augustus, Arminius, and the Slaughter of the Legions in the Teutoburg Forest (W.W. Norton &amp; Company, 2003)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-quinctilius-varus">Publius Quinctilius Varus</a> had been quaestor in 22 BCE, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> in 8 BCE and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> in 7 BCE, and had married the emperor's great-niece. Michael McNally assesses that he was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania">Germania</a> in 7 CE for his political skill, to deal with factionalism among the tribes, not for his military ability. His ruthlessness, including crucifixion of insurgents, was known beyond the empire. He had five legions, of which three were with him at the battle, about 15,000 men, with nine small auxiliary units of about 4,500. How many men were truly under his command is unclear, and estimates generally range from 20,000 to 30,000. McNally puts the likely number of combatants at 17,000 and the civilians at a few hundred.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arminius">Arminius</a> was a nobleman of the Cherusci, taken hostage when Drusus defeated his tribe in 8 BCE, when he was about ten. He was educated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, joined the cavalry, commanded auxiliaries and was serving in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> by 4 CE. On his return he became a trusted adviser to Varus while building an alliance of peoples that had been enemies, probably including the Cherusci, Marsi, Chatti and Bructeri. No Germanic records survive, so the size of his force is a guess: Adrian Murdoch suggests 25,000, Peter Wells gives estimates from 17,000 to 100,000 with 18,000 at the final stage, and Hans Delbrück 20,000 to 30,000. Most of the warriors had no armour and fought with a shield and a hunting spear or axe.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of the Teutoburg Forest&#8221; cites where it covers Publius Quinctilius Varus, Arminius:</p><ul><li>Michael McNally, Teutoburg Forest, AD 9: the destruction of Varus and his legions (Osprey Publishing, 2011)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/segestes"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/segestes.jpg" alt="Segestes" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/segestes">Segestes</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Benjamin_West_(1738-1820)_-_The_Wife_of_Arminius_brought_captive_to_Germanicus_-_RCIN_405683_-_Royal_Collection.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Benjamin West, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> left their winter camps in March and planned a summer camp in Cherusci lands, probably near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minden">Minden</a>. In July <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arminius">Arminius</a> had allies raid the Romans and advised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-quinctilius-varus">Varus</a> to detach troops to put down unrest, which split his force. His father-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segestes">Segestes</a> warned Varus of the treachery, but Varus disbelieved him. In late summer, on the way back to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, Varus heard of a rebellion that Arminius had invented. Arminius proposed a shorter route through the land of the Angrivarii, and on the morning of 7 September the army moved out. That evening Arminius left to gather his troops, taking a quarter of the Roman strength with him.</p><p>On 8 September the Bructeri attacked along a column stretched over 15 to 20 kilometres in thick forest, then withdrew. A downpour followed and Varus had a camp built. The Romans then marched by night into a second trap at the foot of Kalkriese Hill, where a gap of about 100 metres lay between the woods and the edge of the Great Bog, closed by a trench and an earthen wall from which the Germanic warriors attacked. The Romans failed to storm the wall. The legate Numonius Vala rode off with the cavalry and was overtaken and killed. Varus killed himself, the prefect Ceionius surrendered and later took his own life, and Eggius died leading his men.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of the Teutoburg Forest&#8221; cites where it covers Summer operations, Varus's choice, Battle:</p><ul><li>Michael McNally, Teutoburg Forest, AD 9: the destruction of Varus and his legions (Osprey Publishing, 2011)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tiberius"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tiberius.jpg" alt="Tiberius" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:(Venice)_Tiberius_Portrait_of_the_%27Imperium_Maius%27_type_in_Museo_Archeologico_Nazionale.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Didier Descouens, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Roman casualties have been estimated at 15,000 to 20,000 dead, and many officers are said to have killed themselves. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a>, many officers were sacrificed in Germanic religious ceremonies. Others were ransomed, and some common soldiers appear to have been enslaved. Finds at Kalkriese suggest few Germanic losses, though the victors would probably have removed their own dead and buried their battle gear with them.</p><p>The Germanic alliance swept away the Roman forts and garrisons east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. The fort of Aliso, most likely at Haltern am See, held out for many weeks, perhaps a few months, before the garrison under Lucius Caedicius and survivors of the battle broke through to the Rhine. This gave Lucius Nonius Asprenas time to organise the defence of the Rhine with two legions, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> arrived with a new army, so <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arminius">Arminius</a> could not cross into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>.</p><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suetonius">Suetonius</a>, Augustus was so shaken that he butted his head against the walls of his palace, shouting for his legions back. The numbers XVII, XVIII and XIX were never used again. Arminius sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-quinctilius-varus">Varus</a>'s head to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maroboduus">Maroboduus</a> with the offer of an alliance, but Maroboduus declined, sent the head to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> for burial and stayed neutral. Tiberius took effective control and sent three legions to the Rhine to replace the lost ones.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/velleius-paterculus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/velleius-paterculus.jpg" alt="Velleius Paterculus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/velleius-paterculus">Velleius Paterculus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:F16-0039-2_(32528546593).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Medpro, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Roman accounts stress the completeness of the defeat. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/velleius-paterculus">Velleius Paterculus</a> is the only contemporary chronicler to describe the summer, and McNally notes that he had reason to dislike <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-quinctilius-varus">Varus</a> and that later accounts relied on him and took on his bias. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a>, writing some decades afterwards, took a positive view of Varus, while Paterculus implicitly accuses him of corruption. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suetonius">Suetonius</a> tells of Augustus's grief, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> of the sacrifices and of the later burial of the bones, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassius-dio">Cassius Dio</a>, who wrote in the Severan era, says that Varus was carrying out the later stages of colonising a German province.</p><p>For almost 2,000 years the site was unidentified. Several hundred theories followed the rediscovery of Tacitus in the 16th century, and in 1966 Harald Petrikovits grouped them into four: a northern theory, a Lippe theory, a Münsterland theory and a southern theory. In the 19th century the ridge called the Osning near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bielefeld">Bielefeld</a> was renamed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutoburg-forest">Teutoburg Forest</a>. In 1987 Tony Clunn found coins from the reign of Augustus, none later, and lead <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sling">sling</a> bolts at Kalkriese, a site <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodor-mommsen">Theodor Mommsen</a> had suggested. Excavations have found battle debris along a corridor almost 24 kilometres long and little more than 1.6 wide, a long wall of peat turves and sand, human remains and coins countermarked VAR. Kalkriese is now perceived to be an event of the battle. Of some 6,000 pieces of Roman equipment, only one item is clearly Germanic, a spur, which may reflect burial customs and perishable gear.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/germanicus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/germanicus.jpg" alt="Germanicus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/germanicus">Germanicus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Germanicus,_front_-_Getty_Museum_(2021.66).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">J. Paul Getty Museum, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle is considered one of the most important defeats in Roman history. It ended the period of expansion under Augustus and dissuaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> from conquering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania">Germania</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanicus">Germanicus</a> campaigned in 14 to 16 CE, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mattium">Mattium</a>, captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arminius">Arminius</a>'s wife Thusnelda and fought Arminius at the pontes longi and at Idistaviso and the Angrivarian Wall. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> then halted the campaigns. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> claims jealousy of Germanicus lay behind this, and that another summer would have ended the war. Arminius continued to fight, but the conflict remained a stalemate. He was poisoned in 21 CE by fellow Germans, possibly his own family, who feared his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/autocracy">autocracy</a>.</p><p>Some scholars have tempered the view that the defeat alone halted Roman expansion at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. The Rhine could be supplied from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> by river, the region beyond had few villages, little food surplus and a lesser capacity for tribute, and in a cost-benefit analysis the prestige of conquest was outweighed by its cost. The provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania-inferior">Germania Inferior</a> and Germania Superior were formally established in 85 CE.</p><p>From the rediscovery of the Roman sources in the 15th century the battle was given great weight, and Arminius, called Hermann, became a nationalist symbol. The Hermannsdenkmal near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/detmold">Detmold</a> was begun in 1841 and finished in 1875, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a>. Handel's opera Arminio (1736) and Kleist's drama Die Hermannsschlacht (1808) treat the story.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>In September 9 CE an alliance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanic-peoples">Germanic peoples</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arminius">Arminius</a>, a former Roman officer, ambushed and destroyed three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-legion">Roman legions</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-quinctilius-varus">Varus</a>, possibly near Kalkriese. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> lost its forts east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, and campaigns under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanicus">Germanicus</a> did not restore them.</em></p>

